(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-special-topics-nutrition-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Special Topics in Nutrition (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Of hospitalized adults who will experience a nutrition decline during admission, what proportion does lecture cite?","choices":["1% to 5%","10% to 65%","100%","70% to 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"10% to 65% of hospitalized adults will experience a nutrition decline during their admission, per the cited prevalence data. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Per the prevalence data cited, what percentage of hospitalized adults are actually diagnosed with malnutrition (as opposed to merely being at risk or declining)?","choices":["40% to 70%","20% to 50%","Over 90%","5% to 8%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Only 5% to 8% of hospitalized adults are actually diagnosed with malnutrition, a smaller figure than the 20-50% estimated to BE malnourished or the 10-65% who experience a nutrition decline. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed among malnutrition's associated implications in hospitalized patients?","choices":["Decreased overall risk of developing infection","Improved potential for physical rehabilitation","A shorter overall hospital length of stay","Falls, pressure ulcers, and delayed wound healing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Malnutrition is associated with increased falls, pressure ulcers, delayed wound healing, infections, and death, plus decreased rehabilitation potential. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"According to lecture, what degree of lean body mass loss leads to impaired cardiac function, compromised respiratory function, and increased pneumonia risk?","choices":["A loss of 5% lean mass","A loss of 15% lean mass","A loss of 10% lean mass","Loss of 30% lean body mass"],"answer":3,"exp":"A loss of 30% lean body mass leads to impaired cardiac function, compromised respiratory function, increased pneumonia risk, and spontaneous wounds like pressure ulcers. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed among the malnutrition risk factors present BEFORE hospital admission?","choices":["Worsening chronic illness","Need for mealtime assistance","NPO status for a procedure","Polypharmacy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Polypharmacy is listed among the \"before admit\" risk factors, along with older age, nursing home residence, chronic illness, chronic alcohol use, food insecurity, homelessness, gastric bypass history, and mental health conditions. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What two nutrition-screen questions does lecture specifically highlight for identifying at-risk hospitalized adults?","choices":["Current medication list and any known drug allergies","Resting blood pressure reading and current heart rate","Patient's height measurement and current shoe size","Have you lost weight without trying, and has your appetite/eating decreased?"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nutrition screen asks about unintentional weight loss (how much, over what time frame) and decreased appetite/eating. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How is BMI calculated, per the formula given in lecture?","choices":["Weight in kg divided by height in meters squared","Weight in kg multiplied by height in meters","Weight in lbs divided by height in inches","Height in cm divided by weight in kg"],"answer":0,"exp":"BMI = weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared, per the formula given (with conversions: lbs/2.2 = kg, inches × 2.54 = cm). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which malnutrition-related lab marker is specifically noted as being affected by iron deficiency and inflammation, with a half-life of 8-10 days?","choices":["Prealbumin","Transferrin","Retinol binding protein","Albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Transferrin levels are affected by iron deficiency and inflammation, with a half-life of 8-10 days. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Per lecture, how should malnutrition-related labs (albumin, prealbumin, etc.) best be used clinically?","choices":["To trend response to nutrition support, not as a stand-alone diagnostic tool","They should not be checked at all in malnourished patients","As the sole basis used for diagnosing malnutrition outright","Only to rule out an underlying infection in the patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Labs can be used to trend the response to nutrition support, but the lecture explicitly cautions that clinicians \"can NOT rely on labs alone\" to diagnose malnutrition. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a PO (oral) nutrition intervention for malnutrition, short of specialized nutrition support?","choices":["Continuous overnight enteral feeding through a nasoduodenal tube","High calorie/protein meals (double portions) and PO nutrition supplements","Total parenteral nutrition administered through a centrally placed catheter","Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube feeding at home"],"answer":1,"exp":"High calorie/protein meals (such as double portions) and PO nutrition supplements are PO-level interventions, alongside mealtime assistance, texture modification, diet liberalization, and nutrition counseling. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Per lecture, specialized nutrition support (SNS) is generally considered in adults after how many days without adequate intake?","choices":["More than 30 days","5 to 7 days","Fewer than 1 day","1 to 3 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"SNS is generally considered after more than 5-7 days in adults (compared to 3-5 days in children and 1-3 days in infants). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are the three major factors to weigh before starting specialized nutrition support (SNS), per lecture?","choices":["Availability of trained staff, necessary equipment, and appropriate timing within the hospital's daily schedule","Total treatment cost, the patient's insurance coverage, and the receiving hospital's internal policy","Impact of the disease process on nutritional intake, likelihood SNS will improve outcome/quality of life, and preexisting nutritional status","The patient's chronological age, biological sex, ABO blood type, and family history of chronic disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three major factors are the impact of the disease process on nutritional intake, the likelihood that SNS will improve patient outcome or quality of life, and preexisting nutritional status. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Per lecture, is specialized nutrition support (SNS) always obligatory once a patient is malnourished?","choices":["No — SNS is not obligatory and can potentially be harmful in cases of futile care or at the end of life.","No, SNS should never be initiated in any hospitalized patient under any clinical circumstance whatsoever.","Yes, SNS becomes obligatory in every patient once a formal diagnosis of malnutrition is documented.","Yes, providing SNS is a strict legal requirement in all malnourished hospitalized patients nationwide."],"answer":0,"exp":"SNS is explicitly described as not obligatory, and it can potentially be harmful in cases of futile care or end-of-life situations. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which short-term enteral access route is typically used for less than 4-12 weeks, per lecture?","choices":["Surgically or endoscopically placed gastrostomy (GT) tube","Nasogastric (NGT) or nasoduodenal/nasojejunal (NDT/NJT) tube","Combined gastrojejunostomy (GJT) tube requiring surgical placement","Surgically placed jejunostomy (JT) tube for direct small-bowel access"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nasogastric (NGT) or nasoduodenal/nasojejunal (NDT/NJT) tubes are the short-term access route, used for less than 4-12 weeks. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is a key difference between gastric feedings and post-pyloric feedings in enteral nutrition?","choices":["Post-pyloric feedings never require a pump and can be safely delivered as rapid bolus doses several times daily.","Post-pyloric feedings are always more physiologic than gastric feedings and carry a lower aspiration risk overall.","Gastric feedings can be given as bolus feeds, while post-pyloric feedings must run slowly via pump and should NOT be bolused.","Gastric feedings cannot be used for medication administration and must always run continuously via pump."],"answer":2,"exp":"Gastric feedings (GT/PEG/NGT) are the most physiologic and can be given as bolus feeds; post-pyloric feedings (NJT/NDT/GJT/PEJ) must run slowly via a pump over up to 24 hours and should NOT be bolused. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is a cycled enteral feeding schedule, per lecture's example?","choices":["A single large bolus feed delivered once daily, usually overnight while the patient sleeps.","A schedule such as 20 hours on/4 hours off, allowing improved tolerance with time off the pump.","A continuous drip feed that runs for the entire 24-hour period with no pump-free breaks.","A schedule pausing feeds entirely for a full 24 hours between each feeding session."],"answer":1,"exp":"A cycled feed schedule (e.g. 20 hours on, 4 hours off, or 10 hours on/2 off) allows improved tolerance of continuous feeds by giving a few hours off the pump. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of enteral feeding intolerance related to delayed gastric emptying?","choices":["Physical therapy and daily ambulation sessions","Routine annual influenza vaccination","Sedation and narcotic pain medications","Daily oral multivitamin supplementation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sedation and narcotic pain medications are listed causes of delayed gastric emptying and GI dysmotility contributing to enteral feeding intolerance (along with hypothermia, sepsis, gastroparesis, ileus, recent surgery, and trauma). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which of the following is a monitored complication specific to a nasal feeding tube, per lecture?","choices":["Nasal erosion, requiring the tube to be replaced roughly every 30 days with alternating nostrils","Noticeably improved visual acuity within the first few weeks after tube placement","A gradual improvement in the sense of smell over several weeks of tube use","Permanent, irreversible hair loss developing along the tube's nasal insertion path"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nasal erosion is a monitored tube complication; nasal tubes should be replaced roughly every 30 days, alternating nostrils, to help manage this risk. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Per federal long-term care regulations cited in lecture, what right do residents have regarding feeding tube placement?","choices":["Feeding tube placement is mandatory once a resident is malnourished.","Residents have the right to make informed decisions about feeding tube placement.","Only a resident's legal guardian, never the resident, may decide on tube placement.","Facilities may place a feeding tube without resident input if medically indicated."],"answer":1,"exp":"Residents have the right to make informed decisions about feeding tube placement, per the federal regulations described. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does lecture say about enteral feeding in patients with dementia at the end of life?","choices":["It is always medically required in order to prolong the patient's life expectancy.","It reliably eliminates all patient discomfort during the dying process.","It is legally mandated by statute in every single US state.","There is no evidence that enteral feeding is helpful in patients with dementia."],"answer":3,"exp":"Per lecture, there is no evidence that enteral feeding is helpful with patients who have dementia, and nutrition support is not obligatory at the end of life. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What dietary pattern is recommended for chronic liver disease patients to increase tolerance, per lecture?","choices":["Extended fasting between three large daily meals","One very large meal eaten only at dinner","5-7 small meals with a late-night snack","Two large meals daily with strictly no snacking"],"answer":2,"exp":"5-7 small meals with a late-night snack is recommended to increase tolerance and increase protein synthesis post-prandially and overnight. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"If ascites is present in a chronic liver disease patient, what sodium intake is recommended, per lecture?","choices":["No sodium restriction is needed.","Restrict sodium to under 100 mg/day.","Increase sodium intake to compensate for fluid loss.","Reduce sodium intake to 1500-2000 mg/day."],"answer":3,"exp":"If ascites is present, sodium intake should be reduced to 1500-2000 mg/day (and fluid restriction may also be required). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which liver-disease feeding route carries higher risk of complications from ascites, varices, and bleeding, per lecture?","choices":["NGT/NJT","PEG/PEJ/GT","PO (oral) intake","Parenteral nutrition carries no additional risk in this setting."],"answer":1,"exp":"PEG/PEJ/GT placement carries a higher risk of complications from ascites, varices, and bleeding in chronic liver disease. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What carbohydrate-related dietary approach helps manage blood sugar in diabetes, per lecture?","choices":["Favor refined carbohydrates over complex carbohydrates, since they provide quicker bursts of usable energy.","Carbohydrate type doesn't matter, since only total daily calorie count affects blood sugar control.","Favor complex carbohydrates over refined carbohydrates, since they digest more slowly and avoid spikes.","Eliminate all carbohydrates from the diet to avoid any blood sugar spikes entirely."],"answer":2,"exp":"Favoring complex carbohydrates over refined carbohydrates is recommended, since complex carbs are slower to digest and help avoid blood sugar spikes. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What type of dietary fat is emphasized for heart failure patients, per lecture?","choices":["Saturated animal fat","Trans fat, in moderation","Fat intake is irrelevant in heart failure management.","Unsaturated fat, favoring plant over animal sources"],"answer":3,"exp":"Heart failure management emphasizes heart-healthy unsaturated fat, favoring plant sources over animal sources, to help prevent cholesterol buildup. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What nutrition goal applies to cancer patients undergoing treatment, per lecture?","choices":["Restrict calories to below basal needs, since lower intake is believed to slow tumor growth.","Avoid weight loss with adequate calories/protein, small frequent meals, and symptom management.","Encourage gradual weight loss throughout treatment to reduce chemotherapy side effects.","Avoid all oral nutrition supplements in favor of whole-food meals exclusively."],"answer":1,"exp":"The goal is to avoid weight loss during cancer treatment through adequate calories and protein, small frequent meals, and managing symptoms like nausea, mucositis, and taste changes. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which nutrition support route is described as more cost-effective and lower risk of complications, per lecture's enteral-vs-parenteral comparison?","choices":["They are equally cost-effective and equally low-risk.","Neither route has a documented cost or risk difference.","Parenteral nutrition","Enteral nutrition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Enteral nutrition is described as more cost-effective and lower risk of complications compared to parenteral nutrition, which is described as very expensive and high risk of complications. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What components does parenteral nutrition provide via the IV, per lecture?","choices":["Only protein (amino acids) and fat (lipids), without dextrose or micronutrients","Dextrose, protein (amino acids), fat (lipids), vitamins/minerals, electrolytes, and water","Whole blood products, plasma, and platelet concentrates for transfusion","Only dextrose and water, without any added protein or fat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parenteral nutrition provides dextrose, protein (amino acids), fat (lipids), vitamins/minerals, electrolytes, and water — a complete nutritional profile delivered via IV. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"In peripheral parenteral nutrition (PPN), which component does NOT contribute to the solution's osmolarity, per lecture?","choices":["Fat","Electrolytes","Amino acids (protein)","Dextrose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat does not contribute to osmolarity, which is why it's a useful component when working within PPN's limited-osmolarity constraints. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which micronutrients are emphasized for wound healing in trauma patients, per lecture?","choices":["Vitamin C, vitamin A, and zinc","Vitamin K and magnesium","Vitamin D, calcium, and phosphorus","Vitamin B12, folate, and iron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamin C, vitamin A, and zinc are emphasized to support wound healing in trauma patients (consistent with the same micronutrient trio flagged for pressure wounds). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
